WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.170
So how do you actually get there?

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.690
Create a repository from this template.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.870
In one click you get the app, the dev, test and prod branches, the guardrails, and a guide your coding agent follows.

00:00:12.856 --> 00:00:17.866
Open it in GitHub Copilot, VS Code, Claude Code or Cursor, and paste one prompt:

00:00:17.876 --> 00:00:22.106
read AGENTS.md, then set up this project for me.

00:00:22.116 --> 00:00:27.986
The agent installs what's missing, signs you in, registers your dev app, and runs it.

00:00:28.781 --> 00:00:30.091
From there, you just ask.

00:00:30.621 --> 00:00:31.651
Show me the devkit menu.

00:00:32.081 --> 00:00:33.351
Is my project healthy?

00:00:33.661 --> 00:00:35.211
Which environments can I use?

00:00:36.109 --> 00:00:37.099
Create a new app.

00:00:37.429 --> 00:00:38.519
Add a Dataverse table.

00:00:38.889 --> 00:00:40.779
Connect my app to a SharePoint list.

00:00:41.089 --> 00:00:43.519
Can I use this connector in test and prod?

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:45.390
Deploy to test.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:46.850
Share it with my testers.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.170
Roll back production.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:48.930
I need a hotfix.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.470
Add a QA environment.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.850
Help me fix this error.

00:00:53.688 --> 00:00:57.058
Every one of those runs a playbook with the safety checks built in.

00:00:57.488 --> 00:01:00.918
The complexity is still there, but you don't have to worry about it.

00:01:01.635 --> 00:01:09.685
And soon, Copilot Code will bring this same ALM maturity to people who don't have GitHub Copilot or another coding agent.
